   153	## Scripts to reproduce the results and instructions for running them
   154	<a id="scripts"></a>
   155	
   156	### [FuncConstructs-Statistics.r](FuncConstructs-Statistics.r) 
   157	This file contains an R script that you can reuse to re-run all the analyses conducted and discussed in the paper. 
   158	
   159	### [FuncConstructs-Statistics.ipynb](FuncConstructs-Statistics.ipynb)
   160	This file contains the code to re-execute all the analysis conducted in the paper as a Jupyter Notebook (using the R Kernel).
   161	
   162	### [run-analysis.sh](run-analysis.sh)
   163	This script can be used to run the R script `FuncConstructs-Statistics.r` using a Docker container (see Option 1 below) in Unix operating systems.
   164	
   165	### [run-analysis.bat](run-analysis.bat)
   166	This script can be used to run the R script `FuncConstructs-Statistics.r` using a Docker container (see Option 1 below) in Windows operating systems (power shell recommended).
   167	
   168	### [run-jupyter-container.sh](run-jupyter-container.sh)
   169	This script can be used to run a local Jupyter server (with R kernel and all required packages) from a Docker container (see Option 3 below) in Unix operating system.
   170	
   171	### [run-jupyter-container.bat](run-jupyter-container.bat)
   172	This script can be used to run a local Jupyter server (with R kernel and all required packages) from a Docker container (see Option 3 below) in Windows operating systems (power shell recommended).
   173	
   174	
   175	### How to Run the scripts
   176	There are four options to run the scripts. In all cases, one has first to open a shell terminal window (e.g., bash or sh in Unixes) in the replication package directory. For Windows, we suggest to use a Power Shell.
   177	
   178	1. **Running the R script using Dockerized R installation**: this is the simplest option, and it simply requires a running Docker engine. In **Unix (MacOS, Linux)**, to produce the results, one has to run the shell script "run-analysis.sh" (e.g., by typing `sh run-analysis.sh` or simply `./run-analysis.sh` after making it executable).  In **Windows**, one has to run the script "run-analysis.bat" instead (by typing `.\run-analysis.bat`). This script (either .sh or .bat) will:
   179	    - Pull a docker image named `mdipenta/rexp` which contains an R installation with all required packages.
   180	    - Run R from the container created from the image and produce the paper's results under a directory named `results/`.
   181	    
   182	    - **Note:** An alternative would be to run everything from inside the container, after running it in interactive mode. To this aim, please execute the following commands:
   183	          1. In **Unix**: `docker run -v${PWD}:/data --rm -ti --name shell mdipenta/rexp:latest bash` in **Windows**: `docker run -v %cd%:/data --rm -ti --name shell mdipenta/rexp:latest bash`    
   184	          2. `cd data`
   185	          3. `R --no-save < FuncConstructs-Statistics.r`
   186	    After exiting the container, the "results" directory will be again populated with the study results.
   187	        
   188	2. **Running the R script from own R installation**: this option works if one has an R installation already (or wants to use an R installation) without relying on the Docker image. The steps to be followed are:
   189	   - Uncomment the `install.packages(..)` instruction in the first lines of the script. This will allow for the installation of the required packages.
   190	   - Just run, from the current directory, the script `FuncConstructs-Statistics.r`, using the command `Rscript FuncConstructs-Statistics.r` (making sure the directory containing Rscript is in your PATH, this should work fine in Unixes, it might require to modify the PATH environment variable in Windows). Should you experience problems with the first part of the script (installations), try to execute the `install.packages(..)` statement from your R GUI, and then run the script again.
   191	   
   192	3. **Using the Jupyter Notebook using a Dockerized Jupyter lab with R kernel**: this option allows for opening the Jupyter Notebook with all results without having to install Jupyter with the R kernel, nor all the required R packages. The steps required are:
   193	    - Run the `./run-jupyter-container.sh` (**Unix**) or `.\run-jupyter-container.bat` (**Windows**). It will download the `mdipenta/myjupyter` image and run Jupyter lab from it.
   194	    - Open a browser on [localhost:8888](http://localhost:8888) (or if it does not work, [127.0.0.1:8888)](http://127.0.0.1:8888) and, when being asked for a password, type `docker`. 
